---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = 'Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify replacement decisions (2026)';
const desc = 'Yotpo Email & SMS is decommissioned. Evaluate Klaviyo for Shopify continuity, with permission evidence, retired queues, replacement tests and contribution math.';
const faqs = [{"question": "Can I adopt Yotpo Email & SMS now?", "answer": "No. Yotpo announced full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025; its current application notice confirms decommissioning. Evaluate an active replacement."}, {"question": "Does this mean Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty are retired?", "answer": "No. The Email and SMS retirement does not establish retirement of Reviews or Loyalty. Keep those product decisions separate and verify the integrations you need."}, {"question": "When is Klaviyo a useful replacement?", "answer": "When your team wants Shopify event data, email and SMS campaigns and flows in one operating workspace, and your account, destinations and sender registration meet current requirements."}, {"question": "Can I still export from the old dashboard?", "answer": "Do not assume access. The current notice says dashboard and background services are inactive. Inventory retained exports and ask support what evidence or recovery is actually available."}, {"question": "Can my old sending number be moved?", "answer": "Do not promise porting. Ask the receiving provider to verify ownership evidence, number type, destination eligibility and the available transfer process before scheduling a launch."}, {"question": "Should reviews or loyalty membership count as SMS permission?", "answer": "No. Keep channel permission evidence separate from customer identity, purchase history and loyalty membership. Quarantine records with missing or conflicting evidence."}, {"question": "What replaces pending Yotpo journeys?", "answer": "Rebuild only still-relevant jobs from verified events in the chosen active system. Retire old schedules and integrations; never replay an old backlog as fresh demand."}, {"question": "How should costs be compared?", "answer": "Use the same eligible audience, destinations, channel mix and period. Include profile and message charges, carrier fees, implementation, support and retained review or loyalty costs."}, {"question": "What if the replacement test fails?", "answer": "Pause promotional sending, preserve suppressions and investigate with an assigned operator. Roll back to a tested configuration in an active system; the retired sender is not a fallback."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14">
    <nav><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email &amp; SMS</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl mt-8">
      <p class="badge">Retirement and replacement comparison · checked November 25, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email &amp; SMS for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl">This is now a continuity decision. Yotpo Email &amp; SMS is retired; Klaviyo is an active candidate for rebuilding eligible customer journeys.</p>
    </header>
    <TldrBox picks={[
      { label: 'New adoption', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Evaluate unified email and SMS operations against your account and destination requirements.' },
      { label: 'Legacy messaging', tool: 'Yotpo Email & SMS', reason: 'Decommissioned; recover evidence where available and retire old jobs.' },
      { label: 'Separate products', tool: 'Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty', reason: 'Retain when useful; validate data connections independently of messaging.' },
    ]} />
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>What changed, and what still fits</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo’s migration announcement</a> set full deprecation for December 31, 2025. The <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current lifecycle notice</a> confirms that the application is decommissioned and its dashboard and background services are inactive. Old suite comparisons cannot justify new messaging adoption.</p>
      <p>Reviews and Loyalty are separate decisions. A merchant that values its review collection or loyalty program can keep those jobs while choosing an active messaging provider. Verify the event fields, refresh latency and permissions available to the receiving system; owning a review or loyalty record does not grant marketing consent.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> is a useful candidate when one team wants email and SMS campaigns, flows and Shopify-linked profiles. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080407">Shopify integration</a> deserves an event and consent audit before activation. Advanced predictions require qualifying data; do not make a small or newly migrated store depend on them. Simple purchase and preference segments can be a better first release.</p>
      <p>Confirm <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/4402914866843">supported SMS destinations</a>, sender types and registration, then check <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/4404274419355">account setup requirements</a>. Coverage and activation are not universal. If the team needs simpler workflows or a different operating model, include active alternatives from the <a class="link" href="/apps">app catalog</a> in the same test. Yotpo’s historical migration partner recommendations are not proof of your account’s eligibility or best fit.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Klaviyo candidate</th><th>Legacy Yotpo responsibility</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Campaign ownership</td><td>Assign one team to live email and SMS schedules.</td><td>Remove obsolete send ownership from runbooks and integrations.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Customer data</td><td>Validate Shopify events and stable profile joins.</td><td>Inventory retained exports without promising fresh dashboard access.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Consent</td><td>Import only supported, evidenced channel permissions.</td><td>Preserve opt-outs and disclosure history; do not turn blanks into consent.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reviews and loyalty</td><td>Test usable events before depending on them.</td><td>Keep useful product contracts and their costs separate.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <h2>Proposed merchant fixtures, not reported results</h2>
      <p>The following names describe proposed test stores from this comparison’s original scenarios. They are not customers, measured outcomes or platform endorsements. Run each relevant job in the active replacement sandbox with seeded profiles before using a live consented cohort.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Patchwork Denim: one-of-one style drops.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: match style preference and in-stock item; a sale must stop later nudges.</li>
        <li><strong>Bloom & Wick: holiday gifting.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: separate gift deadlines from self-purchase education and expire messages after shipping cutoff.</li>
        <li><strong>Peak Trail Gear: clearance.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: isolate discount-sensitive shoppers from full-price loyalists and compare net contribution.</li>
        <li><strong>Sauce Lab: batch scarcity.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: require fresh stock evidence and cap each launch and scarcity sequence.</li>
        <li><strong>Ember Ceramics: limited kiln release.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: preserve waitlist priority and avoid implying reservation without inventory allocation.</li>
        <li><strong>Ridge Run Coffee: subscription exclusions.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: suppress active subscribers from incompatible flash offers.</li>
        <li><strong>Cipher Audio: high-value launch.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: send education before eligible launch alerts and test a no-discount offer.</li>
        <li><strong>Arena Energy: athlete collaboration.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: keep gift incentives and drop timing consistent across email and SMS.</li>
        <li><strong>Loom & Frame: numbered editions.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: verify remaining edition quantity immediately before scarcity messaging.</li>
        <li><strong>Driftwood Surf Co: seasonal restock.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: use product-specific waitlist intent rather than a full-list replay.</li>
        <li><strong>Noir Collective: VIP streetwear.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: test early access and lower frequency without claiming past lift.</li>
        <li><strong>SoleDrop ATX: sneaker size queues.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: preserve size interest and queue order; stop sends when that size sells out.</li>
        <li><strong>Festival Threads Co: tour merchandising.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: match city, venue date and recipient sending window; expire cancelled events.</li>
        <li><strong>Vinyl Vault Records: collector access.</strong> Proposed acceptance test: validate purchase-based collector eligibility separately from channel permission.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Recover evidence before rebuilding journeys</h2>
      <p>Assign an operator to inventory retained files, integration logs and support responses. Record file provenance and export date. Dashboard recovery, new exports and number porting are unconfirmed until the relevant provider verifies them; the retirement notice gives no promise of continued access. Request a documented answer rather than making a launch depend on recovery.</p>
      <p>Join by a stable Shopify customer ID where available; normalize phone numbers with country codes and verify ambiguous email or phone matches manually. Do not merge two people solely because they share a household number. Reconcile channel-specific status, source, timestamp, disclosure version, destination and opt-out history. Retain suppression evidence even when a profile is excluded from marketing imports. Conflicts and missing permission go to a non-sending quarantine; reviews, orders and loyalty tiers cannot fill the gap.</p>
      <p>Check current provider and destination requirements with qualified advice where needed. Test opt-out processing, reply handling, quiet-hour rules, timezone uncertainty and consent updates separately for each channel. An SMS opt-out must not silently become email permission, and a newly imported positive flag must not override a later suppression. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent controls</a> as an operating checklist.</p>
      <h2>Retire queued jobs and launch without replay</h2>
      <p>Inventory legacy campaigns, delayed steps, webhook consumers, scheduled exports and external retry workers. Disable obsolete producers and revoke unneeded credentials after evidence is preserved. Record which historical jobs were cancelled, expired or deliberately rebuilt. The retired application’s inactive services are not a reason to leave outside schedulers retrying. Never drain the backlog into a new provider.</p>
      <p>Give each new job a stable event ID, customer ID, campaign version and expiry time. Recheck permission, suppression, purchase status and inventory immediately before sending. Test a Shopify purchase between enqueue and delivery: the cart or drop reminder must exit even if the customer still matches a stale segment. Deduplicate repeated webhooks and profile merges across both channels.</p>
      <p>Retry only transient failures with a bounded attempt count and expiry; ambiguous delivery outcomes require status reconciliation before another send. Route exhausted jobs to an operator queue. Test duplicate events, late orders, opt-outs during a delay, missing timezone and sold-out stock. Keep a small consented pilot and a holdout before increasing volume.</p>
      <p>The handoff must name a launch owner and backup, suppression source, monitoring window, alert thresholds and pause control. Preserve receipts and reconciliation counts. If consent conflicts, duplicate sends or missing purchase exits appear, pause the affected journey and inspect the failed fixtures. Roll back to a tested configuration in the active platform or keep sending paused; do not restart Yotpo. Keep suppressions accumulated during the pilot through every rollback.</p>
      <h2>Compare matched cost and net contribution</h2>
      <p>Request a current <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">Klaviyo quote</a> and quotes from other active candidates for the same month, eligible profiles, email volume, SMS destinations, segment counts and sender types. Include carrier fees, overages, setup, migration labor and support. Add retained Reviews or Loyalty charges to every applicable option once; avoid comparing a discontinued Yotpo messaging price to an active plan.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical worksheet, not vendor pricing: for 10,000 eligible customers in each matched test group, treatment net sales are $18,000 and holdout net sales are $15,000. Net sales already subtract discounts and refunds. Incremental net sales are $3,000. Subtract $1,200 incremental goods and fulfillment cost, $500 allocated platform/message/carrier cost and $200 incremental support: net contribution is $1,100, or $0.11 per eligible customer.</p>
      <p>For an alternative with the same $3,000 incremental net sales and $1,200 goods/fulfillment cost, but $650 platform/message/carrier cost and $150 support, contribution is $1,000, or $0.10 per eligible customer. A hypothetical $600 one-time implementation cost makes first-period contributions $500 and $400 respectively if it applies equally. Do not subtract discounts or refunds twice. Use randomized comparable groups, the same observation window and explicit cost allocation; attributed revenue is not incremental lift. Recalculate at peak volume and with lower response before selecting a platform.</p>
      <h2>Decision and operator handoff</h2>
      <p>Choose Klaviyo when the tested event joins, available channels and team workflow support the jobs you actually need. Keep useful Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty functions when their integrations pass. Delay promotional activation when permission or purchase-exit evidence is unresolved. An active replacement that passes a small relevant test is more useful than a feature checklist against a retired sender.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS FAQ" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
